<p t="34" d="3886">- This might be the most
ambitious long shot precision</p>
<p t="3920" d="2240">experiment that I&#39;ve ever seen.</p>
<p t="6160" d="2880">Scientists have put one
atomic clock down in Boulder,</p>
<p t="9040" d="3200">Colorado, and one up in this
observatory 4,000 meters</p>
<p t="12240" d="1360">above sea level, and they&#39;re going to try</p>
<p t="13600" d="2160">and measure the difference
in time dilation</p>
<p t="15760" d="2240">according to general relativity.</p>
<p t="18000" d="3120">Because up here, time itself
should be passing faster</p>
<p t="21120" d="2040">relative to down in Boulder.</p>
<p t="23160" d="2280">- If you go into a science center,</p>
<p t="25440" d="2320">often they will explain
gravity as the bending</p>
<p t="27760" d="2240">of space time, and what they
will have is a little funnel</p>
<p t="30000" d="2640">where the particle goes around the funnel,</p>
<p t="32640" d="2240">and that&#39;s meant to give you an impression</p>
<p t="34880" d="2320">of why the earth goes around the sun.</p>
<p t="37200" d="1920">But that&#39;s not actually what happens.</p>
<p t="39120" d="2720">It&#39;s not as much that
space is curving, it&#39;s</p>
</body>
</timedtext>
